<%- include('partials/head') %>

<section class="hero" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
  <div class="hero-copy">
    <h1 id="hero-title">No bot gets in without a human saying yes.</h1>
    <p class="hero-lede">
      BotApprove removes every bot that joins your server and holds it for review. It does not
      matter who invited it. The owner gets no exception, because a compromised owner account is
      exactly how servers get wiped.
    </p>
    <% if (typeof serverCount !== 'undefined' && serverCount) { %>
      <p class="hero-count">
        Screening bots in <strong><%= serverCount.toLocaleString('en-US') %></strong>
        server<%= serverCount === 1 ? '' : 's' %>.
      </p>
    <% } %>
    <div class="cta">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="<%= inviteUrl %>">Add to Discord</a>
      <a class="btn" href="/login">Sign in to the dashboard</a>
    </div>
    <p class="hint">
      Free to add and open source, with no trial countdown. Signing in asks for your identity and
      your server list, nothing else.
    </p>
  </div>

  <figure class="exhibit">
    <article class="approval" data-demo data-state="awaiting" aria-label="Example approval card">
      <div class="ap-top">
        <span class="badge warn" data-status>
          <svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-wait"></use></svg>
          <span>Kicked · awaiting decision</span>
        </span>
        <span class="example-tag">Example</span>
      </div>
      <div class="ap-head">
        <span class="ap-avatar" aria-hidden="true">E</span>
        <div>
          <p class="ap-title">Bot kicked, approval required</p>
          <p class="ap-desc">
            <strong>Example Bot#0000</strong> tried to join and was removed pending review.
            Nothing is trusted by default here, including bots invited by the owner.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <dl class="ap-fields">
        <div><dt>Bot</dt><dd>Example Bot#0000<br><code>123456789012345678</code></dd></div>
        <div><dt>Account created</dt><dd>2 days ago</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Discord verified</dt><dd>Not verified</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Added by</dt><dd><span class="placeholder">[the member who invited it]</span></dd></div>
        <div><dt>Removed from server</dt><dd>Yes</dd></div>
        <div class="wide">
          <dt>Permissions it was granted (post-join)</dt>
          <dd>
            Roles: <code>Example Bot</code>
            <span class="perm-flag">
              <svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-danger"></use></svg>
              Administrator, full control of the server
            </span>
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
      <p class="ap-foot">Approval #0 · Your server</p>
      <div class="stamp stamp-face" hidden aria-hidden="true"></div>
    </article>

    <div class="ap-controls">
      <p class="ap-outcome" role="status"></p>
    </div>

    <div class="outcomes-static">
      <div class="os-item">
        <p class="os-btn">Approve &amp; whitelist</p>
        <span class="stamp-face stamp-mini">Whitelisted</span>
        <p>Whitelisted. It doesn't come back by itself; someone re-invites it by hand.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="os-item">
        <p class="os-btn">Deny</p>
        <span class="stamp-face stamp-mini">Denied</span>
        <p>Denied. It is not whitelisted, and BotApprove DMs whoever added it.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="os-item">
        <p class="os-btn">Deny + confirm nuke bot</p>
        <span class="stamp-face stamp-mini crit">
          <svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-crit"></use></svg>
          Kicked on sight
        </span>
        <p>Kicked on sight from now on. Where auto-ban is on, BotApprove bans whoever invited it.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <figcaption>
      An example card for a made-up bot. BotApprove posts the real one in your approval channel
      after the kick, never before, so nothing sits in your server while it gathers these details.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<section class="band" aria-labelledby="fails-title">
  <div class="band-head">
    <h2 id="fails-title">How it fails</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      Every failure ends with BotApprove not trusting the bot. The label on each row says whether
      that outcome is safe on its own or needs a person to look.
    </p>
  </div>
  <ul class="ledger">
    <li>
      <span class="badge ok"><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-ok"></use></svg>Safe on its own</span>
      <p class="when">Something errors</p>
      <p class="what">BotApprove kicks the bot. A failed check is never mistaken for a passed one.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="badge ok"><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-ok"></use></svg>Safe on its own</span>
      <p class="when">Name matches your blocklist</p>
      <p class="what">BotApprove stops it before reading the whitelist, so a stolen approval cannot be reused.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="badge ok"><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-ok"></use></svg>Safe on its own</span>
      <p class="when">Bot carries a verified badge</p>
      <p class="what">The approver sees the badge. BotApprove never trusts it, because verified apps get compromised too.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="badge ok"><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-ok"></use></svg>Safe on its own</span>
      <p class="when">Subscription lapses</p>
      <p class="what">The gate keeps running. Screening is free forever and the bot never leaves.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="badge warn"><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-warn"></use></svg>Needs a human</span>
      <p class="when">Someone weakens BotApprove</p>
      <p class="what">BotApprove reports a lost permission, or being moved down the role list, as a probable attack rather than a settings change.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="badge crit"><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-crit"></use></svg>Critical</span>
      <p class="when">A kick fails</p>
      <p class="what">BotApprove escalates it as critical. It means an unapproved bot is in your server right now.</p>
    </li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="band split" aria-labelledby="src-title">
  <div class="split-lead">
    <h2 id="src-title">Open source, so you can check</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      BotApprove asks for permission to kick and ban people in your server. You should not have to
      take anyone's word for what it does with that. All of it is on GitHub under AGPL-3.0.
    </p>
    <% if (repoUrl) { %>
      <a class="btn" href="<%= repoUrl %>" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">
        <svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-github"></use></svg>
        Read the source on GitHub
      </a>
    <% } %>
  </div>
  <dl class="points">
    <div>
      <dt>Read the part that decides</dt>
      <dd>
        <code>src/services/botJoinPipeline.js</code> is the whole gate, in the order it runs.
        Every claim on this page is a few hundred lines you can check.
      </dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Run it yourself</dt>
      <dd>
        Node and a SQLite file, no external services. If you would rather not trust a hosted bot
        with your server, host your own.
      </dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Changes have to be published</dt>
      <dd>
        AGPL means anyone running a modified BotApprove as a service has to publish what they
        changed. A quietly weakened fork cannot stay quiet.
      </dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Report a flaw privately</dt>
      <dd>
        Security reports go to the operator directly, not into a public issue where they would be
        a working exploit against every server until a fix ships.
      </dd>
    </div>
  </dl>
</section>

<%- include('partials/suite') %>

<section class="band" aria-labelledby="perm-title">
  <div class="band-head">
    <h2 id="perm-title">What it asks for</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      BotApprove asks for the permissions it uses and nothing more. It never requests the Message
      Content intent, so it cannot read your messages.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="manifest">
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Permission</th><th scope="col">What BotApprove uses it for</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Kick Members, Ban Members</th>
          <td>Removing an unapproved bot, and banning one on the shared threat list.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">View Audit Log</th>
          <td>Reading who invited a bot. This is the only way to know after the fact.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">View Messages, Send Messages, Embed Links, Attach Files</th>
          <td>Posting the approval card in your chosen channel, with the bot's avatar attached.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Read Message History, Change Nickname</th>
          <td>Editing its own approval message after a restart, and wearing the name you pick.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="cta">
    <a class="btn btn-primary" href="<%= inviteUrl %>">Add to Discord</a>
    <% if (supportUrl) { %>
      <a class="btn" href="<%= supportUrl %>" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Ask in the support server</a>
    <% } %>
  </div>
  <p class="hint">
    After adding it, drag the BotApprove role above where new bots land, then run
    <code>/config notify-channel</code> to pick where approvals appear.
  </p>
</section>

<%- include('partials/foot') %>
